// Café Dulce: Systems Redesign project detail
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Café Dulce: Systems Redesign - Maverick Huynh</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <nav class="nav">
      <span class="nav-text">
        Sophomore at USC based in Los Angeles /
        <a href="/" class="nav-link">home</a> /
        <a href="/#about" class="nav-link">about</a>
      </span>
    </nav>

    <main class="project-page">
      <a href="/#projects" class="back-link">← Projects</a>

      <header class="project-header">
        <div class="project-header-top">
          <h1 class="project-title">Café Dulce: Systems Redesign</h1>
          <span class="project-date">USC Course Project</span>
        </div>
        <div class="project-intro">
          <p>
            Café Dulce is a popular campus café with a familiar problem: peak-hour congestion, long wait times, and an ordering flow that creates bottlenecks at the counter. For a systems engineering team project, we treated the café like a production system, mapping the current state, identifying constraints, and redesigning the experience around throughput and clarity.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond recommendations on paper, we also built a mobile order MVP to show how shifting demand off the physical line could cut congestion and give staff a cleaner queue to work from. The goal wasn't just a prettier layout, it was a measurable improvement to how people move through the space.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="project-meta glass-panel">
        <div class="meta-row">
          <span class="meta-label">Role</span>
          <span class="meta-value">Systems Engineering Team Member</span>
        </div>
        <div class="meta-row">
          <span class="meta-label">Focus</span>
          <span class="meta-value">Ordering flow, layout, mobile order MVP</span>
        </div>
        <div class="meta-row">
          <span class="meta-label">Type</span>
          <span class="meta-value">Systems Engineering · Team Project</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="project-section glass-panel">
        <div class="section-logo">
          <img src="/images/images-Picsart-BackgroundRemover.png" alt="Café Dulce: Systems Redesign" />
        </div>
        <h2 class="section-title">What we worked on</h2>
        <p>
          We broke the café into stages: arrival, order, payment, pickup, and seating, and studied where queues formed and why. From there, we proposed layout changes to reduce cross-traffic and make the pickup path clearer, then scoped a mobile ordering MVP so customers could place orders before they hit the counter.
        </p>
        <p>
          The MVP was meant to prove the concept: fewer people standing still in the ordering zone, more predictable prep for staff, and a system that scales better during rush without requiring a full rebuild of the café.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="project-section glass-panel">
        <h2 class="section-title">Why it matters</h2>
        <p>
          This project was my first real introduction to applying systems thinking to something in the real world, and it genuinely changed how I see things. I can't walk into a store, a restaurant, or a car wash anymore without noticing the system running underneath it: where people bottleneck, how things flow, what's working and what isn't.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's a small shift, but it's stuck with me. Good engineering isn't always about building something new. Sometimes it's about actually seeing the process people already live inside and making it work better.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="project-section glass-panel visuals-section">
        <div class="visuals-atmosphere" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="visuals-blob visuals-blob-1"></span>
          <span class="visuals-blob visuals-blob-2"></span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="section-title">Project visuals</h2>
        <p>
          From the peak-hour line outside Dulce to the causal loops, process flows, and redesigned layout that shaped our proposal.
        </p>
        <div class="visuals-grid">
          <figure class="visual-card">
            <img
              src="/images/cafe-dulce/exterior-line.png"
              alt="Long line of customers outside Café Dulce during peak hours"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>Peak-hour congestion outside Café Dulce</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="visual-card">
            <img
              src="/images/cafe-dulce/causal-loop.png"
              alt="Causal loop diagram of Dulce's current ordering system"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>Current ordering loop: wait time and overcrowding</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="visual-card visual-card-wide">
            <img
              src="/images/cafe-dulce/process-flow.png"
              alt="Swimlane process flow for the mobile ordering experience"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>Mobile order process flow across customer, prep, and pickup</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="visual-card visual-card-wide">
            <img
              src="/images/cafe-dulce/layout-after.png"
              alt="Proposed Café Dulce layout after redesign with mobile pickup path"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>Proposed layout after: clearer flow and dedicated mobile pickup</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="project-section glass-panel resources-panel">
        <h2 class="section-title">Full report and demo</h2>
        <p>
          Want the full write-up or a walkthrough of the mobile ordering MVP? Both are linked below.
        </p>
        <div class="resource-links">
          <a
            class="resource-link"
            href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/16isaXh5Tw4_UPKxjxP7AJEYG4J0Wq7oa/view?usp=sharing"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            <span class="resource-link-label">Full team report</span>
            <span class="resource-link-meta">PDF</span>
          </a>
          <a
            class="resource-link"
            href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pqCGaP-t-PvI53qyIFjkE2OrMSvHpZrG/view?usp=sharing"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            <span class="resource-link-label">Mobile ordering MVP demo</span>
            <span class="resource-link-meta">Download MP4</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

